Sometimes people ask about statues.
Here's some info I gleaned from the code.
If anyone has more to offer, please let's hear it.

SPOILER ALERT


"Common" uses for statues:
  Wield them and then use them to force open chests.
  Break them open with a pickaxe, force bolt spell or wand of
    striking to see if they contain a spellbook.

"Wierd" uses(?):
  Use for weight in the "cursed bag of holding" abuse.
  Wield as a weapon.
  etc.

A statue's weight is 1.5 times the weight of the corresponding
monster. 

When created (such as when a new level is generated), statues have a
probability of containing a spellbook based on the dungeon level as
follows:

     Level         Probability
                   of Spellbook

     1,2,3         0
     4,5           1/12 (8.3%)
     6,7           2/13 (15.4%)
     8,9           3/14 (21.4%)
     10,11         4/15 (26.7%)
     12,13         5/16 (31.3%)
      ...            ...
     20,21         9/20 (45.0%)
      ...            ...
     30,31         14/25 (56.0%)
      ...            ...
     50            24/35 (68.6%)

The exceptions are:
  Monsters turned to stone by being hit with a cockatrice corpse;
  Statues with "player" names (such as are encountered on the
    medusa level or a bones level).
These statues will definitely NOT contain a spellbook!

However, you can use a wand of polymorph on boulders and statues of
the types noted in the exception above with a 90% probability of
changing them into a (different) statue (10% chance of polymorphing
into a boulder).  When so polymorphed, the probabilities for
spellbooks apply.  So, after using a cockatrice corpse to clean out a
treasure zoo, beehive, throne room, etc., pile all the statues
together, polymorph them, and then break them open with your pickaxe. 
(You could even carry the statues down deeper into the dungeon before
polymorphing them in order to increase the odds of getting spellbooks.
However, some might be too heavy to carry.)

The cursed/blessed status of a spellbook from a statue will be the
same as that of the statue (i.e., blessed statues yield blessed
spellbooks, etc.). 



